Extracted from that commit, with the following local integration changes:
HULL_QUICKJS_FULL_POINTERS disables the 64-bit 47-bit NaN-boxing branch.
Hull enables it for both TRUEOS and host fixtures so JSValue retains full-width
native pointers. TRUEOS guest heaps use canonical high-half virtual addresses.
The existing upstream full-pointer JSValue implementation is otherwise unchanged.

Hull module preparation in quickjs.c/quickjs.h:
- JS_HullModuleRequests uses an ephemeral context and the upstream parser to
  return static import/export-from requests without resolving or executing code.
  JS_EVAL_FLAG_HULL_NO_RESOLVE is an internal host compile-only flag (bit 8).
  Metadata defines own array elements, avoiding guest prototype setters.
- JS_HullSetImportWaiter lets the adapter retain dynamic import Promise resolvers
  after the standard specifier/options conversion. Default behavior is unchanged
  unless the host installs this hook. Actual linking/evaluation/namespace creation
  remains JS_LoadModule; the adapter resumes it after source graph preparation.
- The internal async-function Promise consumed by synchronous module execution
  is marked handled, so a host rejection tracker reports the public import/module
  failure once and does not report an error the guest has caught.

The C adapter in build.rs owns prepared source bodies, queued import resolvers
and bounded rejection diagnostics. None of these add guest globals or perform I/O.
